{
  "source": "videos/tokens-v6.mp4",
  "duration": 217.6,
  "segments": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 5.24,
      "text": "We use AI by having a conversation. You ask a question in plain English, like,"
    },
    {
      "start": 5.66,
      "end": 9.54,
      "text": "what's the best Avengers movie? And the AI replies with a detailed answer,"
    },
    {
      "start": 9.86,
      "end": 16.96,
      "text": "just like a person would. It runs on math. It only processes numbers. So how does the system"
    },
    {
      "start": 16.96,
      "end": 22.16,
      "text": "translate your written words into a mathematical language it can calculate? The most obvious"
    },
    {
      "start": 22.16,
      "end": 26.14,
      "text": "approach would be to assign a unique number to every single word in the dictionary."
    },
    {
      "start": 26.14,
      "end": 32.32,
      "text": "But that system breaks down almost immediately. People constantly invent new slang, make typos,"
    },
    {
      "start": 32.68,
      "end": 38.0,
      "text": "and type in code or emojis. A list of a million words still wouldn't cover everything a person"
    },
    {
      "start": 38.0,
      "end": 43.58,
      "text": "might type into a chat box. Instead of trying to memorize every possible whole word,"
    },
    {
      "start": 44.18,
      "end": 49.76,
      "text": "AI uses reusable text pieces called tokens. Think of them as the basic building blocks for language."
    },
    {
      "start": 50.32,
      "end": 54.2,
      "text": "A token can be a whole word, or it can be just a small fragment of one."
    },
    {
      "start": 54.2,
      "end": 60.68,
      "text": "Take a multi-syllable word like unbelievable. The AI doesn't view this as a single unit."
    },
    {
      "start": 61.06,
      "end": 68.36,
      "text": "It splits the word into three distinct chunks, un, belie, and vable. This system is incredibly"
    },
    {
      "start": 68.36,
      "end": 74.8,
      "text": "efficient because those fragments are reusable. That exact same un prefix can be combined with"
    },
    {
      "start": 74.8,
      "end": 81.76,
      "text": "other pieces to build unusual or unmatchable. The AI's vocabulary doesn't need a brand new entry"
    },
    {
      "start": 81.76,
      "end": 88.68,
      "text": "for every single variation of a word. Before you ever open a chat window, engineers use programs"
    },
    {
      "start": 88.68,
      "end": 94.92,
      "text": "to analyze massive collections of text. This analysis determines exactly how words will be split"
    },
    {
      "start": 94.92,
      "end": 101.06,
      "text": "and establishes the AI's permanent vocabulary list. These vocabularies are large but highly"
    },
    {
      "start": 101.06,
      "end": 108.74,
      "text": "optimized. ChatGPT holds about 200,000 distinct tokens, while Google's Gemini uses about 256,000"
    },
    {
      "start": 108.74,
      "end": 114.88,
      "text": "to cover human communication. Every single token on that list gets assigned its own specific number"
    },
    {
      "start": 114.88,
      "end": 120.4,
      "text": "called a token ID. It acts like an exact address in the AI's vocabulary catalog."
    },
    {
      "start": 121.9,
      "end": 126.64,
      "text": "So when you type a message and hit send, a program called a tokenizer steps in."
    },
    {
      "start": 127.12,
      "end": 132.2,
      "text": "It breaks your text down into those reusable chunks and then looks up the numerical ID for"
    },
    {
      "start": 132.2,
      "end": 139.82,
      "text": "each one. Unbelievable instantly becomes the numbers 359, 32898, and 24694."
    },
    {
      "start": 140.86,
      "end": 146.02,
      "text": "But we have to make a strict distinction here. The tokenizer converts the written word cat"
    },
    {
      "start": 146.02,
      "end": 153.7,
      "text": "into ID number 4719. That number only identifies the specific text fragment. To the AI at this"
    },
    {
      "start": 153.7,
      "end": 159.96,
      "text": "precise step in the process, 4719 tells it absolutely nothing about what a cat actually is."
    },
    {
      "start": 159.96,
      "end": 166.2,
      "text": "It is just a catalog number, not a mathematical representation of fur, whiskers, or a small animal."
    },
    {
      "start": 167.62,
      "end": 173.8,
      "text": "This chart shows how a tokenizer handles everything. ChatGPT is chopped into three tokens,"
    },
    {
      "start": 174.7,
      "end": 181.78,
      "text": "chat, g, and pt. In iHeartAI, spaces aren't ignored. They are actively bundled into the"
    },
    {
      "start": 181.78,
      "end": 187.64,
      "text": "tokens themselves, totaling three distinct chunks. Finally, a standard web address is"
    },
    {
      "start": 187.64,
      "end": 194.22,
      "text": "methodically sliced into eight smaller bits. When the AI has calculated its answer, the journey"
    },
    {
      "start": 194.22,
      "end": 200.1,
      "text": "happens in reverse. The model outputs a sequence of token IDs, and the tokenizer converts those"
    },
    {
      "start": 200.1,
      "end": 204.7,
      "text": "numbers back into their corresponding text fragments. Those fragments are then stitched"
    },
    {
      "start": 204.7,
      "end": 208.82,
      "text": "tightly together, creating the final coherent sentences that appear on your screen."
    },
    {
      "start": 210.44,
      "end": 214.28,
      "text": "Words become numbers. That lets AI work with your language using math."
    }
  ],
